If your goal is analytical (OLAP) queries across distributed PostgreSQL data:

Trino/PrestoDB: Query engine that connects to multiple PostgreSQL sources and performs columnar processing itself (doesn't store columnar in Postgres)
ClickHouse MaterializedPostgreSQL engine: Replicates from multiple PostgreSQL sources into ClickHouse's native columnar format
Apache Doris/StarRocks: MPP databases that can query PostgreSQL external tables using columnar execution
Summary Table
Project	Distributed	Columnar	Notes
Greenplum	Yes	Yes	Fork, not standard Postgres
Citus	Yes	No	Columnar deprecated
TimescaleDB	No (discontinued)	Yes	Single-node focus now
Postgres-XL	Yes	No	Maintenance mode
DuckDB (attached Postgres)	No	Yes	Single-node analytics
Bottom line: If you need a production system today that is both columnar and distributed across PostgreSQL-compatible nodes, Greenplum is essentially your only option, despite it being an integrated system rather than a loose federation of PostgreSQL servers. For a true "many separate PostgreSQL servers with columnar tables" architecture, you would need to build custom middleware or use a federated query engine like Trino.
